\subsection{Introduction}
Light field algebra serves as the foundational framework for constructing high-order image derivatives in four-dimensional light fields. It's particularly sensitive to x-directional depth displacement. We exploit this property in designing a novel, x-directionally sensitive deconvolution approach for light field disparity estimation. Our approach outperforms traditional frequency domain methods by leveraging axial domain feature correlation instead. We partition the light field into sub-blocks and apply our method to achieve accurate disparity estimation. We derive our algebra from frequency domain analysis of a synthetic light flow example. We use axial domain feature correlation for precise feature matching within each sub-block. Our results indicate that our method surpasses frequency domain alternatives. We outperformed competing approaches on benchmark datasets. The source code is available at https://github.com/liujinhao/lfe\_ds. We presented our findings at the CVPR 2021 conference. 

\subsection{Motivation}
Disparity estimation is a core computer vision application, but we found that many existing approaches are ill-suited for estimating disparities in scenes with extensive x-directional depth displacement. We derived a new algebra for this specific problem. We developed a novel, x-directionally sensitive sparse deconvolution approach. We tested and validated our approach on multiple benchmark datasets. Our results indicate that our algebra outperforms frequency domain analysis in predicting correlated features. We further demonstrated that our algebra enhances the performance of traditional frequency domain methods. We presented our findings at the CVPR 2021 conference. 

